const USE={'1-1':'The proton. Its spin makes hydrogen nuclei the signal in NMR spectroscopy and in hospital MRI scans.','1-2':'Deuterium. Used in heavy water, NMR solvents and as a fuel in fusion experiments.','1-3':'Tritium. A fusion fuel, a tracer for water movement, and the glow in self-lit exit signs and watch dials.','2-3':'Cools dilution refrigerators to within thousandths of a degree of absolute zero, and detects neutrons.','2-4':'The alpha particle. Liquid helium-4 cools MRI magnets and particle accelerators.','3-6':'Breeds tritium for fusion reactors and detects neutrons.','4-10':'Made by cosmic rays hitting rock. Measures how long a surface has been exposed, such as dating glacier retreat.','5-10':'Captures neutrons strongly. Used in reactor control rods and boron neutron capture cancer therapy.','6-12':'Defines the atomic mass unit: one carbon-12 atom is exactly 12 u.','6-13':'Carbon-13 NMR maps the carbon skeleton of molecules, and it traces metabolism without radiation.','6-14':'Radiocarbon dating of anything that was once alive, back to about 50,000 years.','7-15':'Stable tracer for nitrogen in fertilizer, ecology and protein NMR.','8-15':'Short-lived positron emitter for PET scans of blood flow.','8-18':'Ratios of oxygen-18 to oxygen-16 in ice cores and shells record past temperatures. Also the starting material for fluorine-18.','9-18':'The most used PET tracer, in FDG, which lights up cancer and brain activity. Half-life about 110 minutes.','11-22':'Positron source used to calibrate PET scanners and study materials.','13-26':'Its decay heated early asteroids. Used to date events in the early Solar System from meteorites.','14-28':'Almost perfect silicon-28 spheres were used to count atoms for the 2019 redefinition of the kilogram.','15-31':'Phosphorus-31 NMR tracks energy molecules like ATP inside living tissue.','15-32':'Labels DNA and RNA in molecular biology experiments.','16-35':'Labels proteins in biochemistry experiments.','17-36':'Dates very old groundwater, up to about a million years.','18-40':'The end product of potassium-40 decay, measured in potassium–argon dating.','19-40':'Potassium–argon dating of volcanic rock, which dated early human fossils. Also the main natural radioactivity in the human body.','20-48':'The calcium-48 beam that made elements 114 to 118.','22-50':'The titanium-50 beam Berkeley Lab plans to use in its search for element 120.','23-51':'The vanadium-51 beam RIKEN is using in its search for element 119.','24-51':'Labels red blood cells to measure their lifespan.','26-56':'One of the most tightly bound nuclei. Fusion in stars stops paying off around iron and nickel.','26-57':'Mössbauer spectroscopy, which identified iron minerals on Mars from the Spirit and Opportunity rovers.','26-60':'Found in deep-sea crust, it records nearby supernovae from the last few million years.','27-60':'Strong gamma source for radiotherapy, sterilizing medical equipment and food irradiation.','28-62':'Has the highest binding energy per nucleon of any nucleus.','28-63':'Powers electron-capture detectors used to find trace pollutants.','29-64':'PET imaging and cancer therapy research.','30-70':'The zinc-70 beam that made nihonium (element 113).','31-68':'PET imaging of neuroendocrine and prostate cancers.','32-76':'Used in searches for neutrinoless double beta decay, which would show whether neutrinos are their own antiparticles.','36-81':'Dates ancient groundwater and ice.','36-85':'Leak detection and a tracer for nuclear fuel reprocessing.','37-87':'Rubidium–strontium dating of rocks and meteorites.','38-87':'Strontium-87 optical lattice clocks are among the most precise clocks ever built. Strontium isotope ratios also trace where ancient people and animals lived.','38-90':'Fission product in fallout, and a power source for remote generators.','39-90':'Tiny radioactive beads treat liver cancer (radioembolization).','42-99':'Parent of technetium-99m. Hospitals receive it in generators and draw off technetium as needed.','43-99':'Its excited form, technetium-99m (half-life about 6 hours), is the most used medical imaging isotope, in tens of millions of scans a year. The ground state shown here lasts far longer.','53-123':'Thyroid imaging.','53-125':'Small seeds implanted to treat prostate cancer (brachytherapy).','53-131':'Treats overactive thyroid and thyroid cancer. Half-life about 8 days.','54-129':'Hyperpolarized xenon-129 MRI images airflow in the lungs.','55-133':'Defines the second: 9,192,631,770 oscillations of caesium-133 in atomic clocks.','55-137':'Fission product used in industrial gauges, and a tracer for soil erosion and fallout.','62-147':'Samarium–neodymium dating of the oldest rocks.','64-157':'Absorbs thermal neutrons more strongly than any other stable isotope. Used in reactor control.','70-171':'Ytterbium-171 optical lattice clocks rival strontium clocks in precision.','71-176':'Lutetium–hafnium dating of the early Earth.','71-177':'Targeted radiotherapy for neuroendocrine and prostate cancers.','77-192':'Industrial radiography of welds, and brachytherapy.','81-201':'Heart imaging (thallium stress test).','82-206':'End product of uranium-238 decay, used in uranium–lead dating.','82-207':'End product of uranium-235 decay. Uranium–lead dating gives the 4.5-billion-year age of the Earth.','82-210':'Dates lake and ocean sediments from the last 150 years.','83-209':'Technically radioactive, with a half-life about a billion times the age of the universe.','84-210':'Intense alpha emitter, used in anti-static brushes.','86-222':'Radon gas in homes, the second leading cause of lung cancer. Also studied as an earthquake precursor.','88-223':'Xofigo, which treats prostate cancer that has spread to bone.','88-226':'Studied by Marie Curie, and the first isotope used in radiotherapy.','89-225':'Targeted alpha therapy for advanced cancers.','90-229':'Has an unusually low-energy nuclear transition that was excited with a laser in 2024, a step toward a nuclear clock.','90-232':'Fuel for proposed thorium reactors.','92-235':'The fissile isotope that fuels most nuclear reactors. Only 0.72% of natural uranium.','92-238':'Uranium–lead dating, and the most common uranium isotope.','94-238':'Powers radioisotope generators on Voyager, Curiosity and Perseverance.','94-239':'Fissile. Made in reactors and used as reactor fuel.','95-241':'The ionization source in most household smoke detectors.','96-244':'Alpha source in rover X-ray spectrometers that measured rock chemistry on Mars.','96-248':'The curium-248 target in RIKEN’s search for element 119.','97-249':'The berkelium-249 target that made tennessine (element 117).','98-249':'The californium target that made oganesson, and the planned target for element 120.','98-252':'Portable neutron source for starting reactors and scanning cargo.'};
